=== 2026-11-02 Stage 1: sources ===
Target 1: De Neve, Vanden Kerchove, Colle, Tavernier, Pickavet, "On the chromatic
number of the plane with two forbidden distances", Amer. Math. Monthly 132(10)
(2025) 1007-1022, DOI 10.1080/00029890.2025.2559554.
- Open-access accepted manuscript from UGent biblio (handle 1854/LU-01K94Y89D25FFMV0FYQ44CTS2P),
  15 pp., downloaded to runs/papers/deNeve2025_acc.pdf, text in runs/papers/deNeve2025.txt.
- Appendix "Vertex coordinates of smallest graphs", pp. 14-15; Table 1 (p. 15) gives all
  33 coordinates for d = sqrt(3) in the notation [a0 a1 a2 a3], meaning
  (x,y) = ((a0+a1*sqrt5)/8, (a2*sqrt3+a3*sqrt15)/8). Edges are NOT tabulated.
- Figure 11 (p. 12) draws the same graph; caption says "33 vertices and 113 edges".
- Definition 1 (p. 1): an edge exists iff the distance is in D. Hence the edge list is
  recoverable from the coordinates with D = {1, sqrt(3)}.
  Definitional note: "2-distance graph" here means two possible EDGE lengths, not a
  two-distance point set.
- Table 2 (p. 15) separately gives the 117-vertex graph for d = (sqrt2+sqrt6)/2; not used.

Target 2 (checked, not used): Parts, "A small 6-chromatic two-distance graph in the plane",
arXiv:2010.12656v2, Geombinatorics 29/3 (2020) 111-115.
- No coordinates published.
- Explicit edge lists for G16 on p. 3: 28 edges of length 1 and 28 of length d.
- G16 is 5-chromatic (contains 5-cliques) and every 5-colouring forces vertices 1 and 16
  to share a colour (proof p. 4). The 6-chromatic graph is G31: two copies of G16 rotated
  about the common vertex 1 by arccos((95+sqrt5)/100), making the two copies of 16 unit.
- G16 is a subgraph of G126 = fivefold Minkowski sum of the regular pentagon with side 1,
  but no labelling of vertices 1..16 by coordinates is given, so it is not reproducible
  without solving for an embedding. Superseded by target 1.

Target 3 (checked, not used): Exoo-Ismailescu, arXiv:1909.13177, 426 vertices,
2009 unit + 892 length-2 edges. The paper gives the construction; coordinate and
adjacency data are not in the paper, only by reference to
http://cs.indstate.edu/ge/ExooIsmailescuData/{vertices,matrix}.1.2.graph.txt (refs [2],[3]).

=== Stage 2: transcription ===
Table 1 (33 rows of four integers) transcribed verbatim into
problems/hadwiger_nelson/two_distance.py as TABLE_1.
Edge set rebuilt exactly as Definition 1 prescribes: every pair at distance 1 or sqrt(3).

=== Stage 3: exact verification ===
- 33 distinct points, no coincident vertices.
- 113 edges = 81 at distance 1 + 32 at distance sqrt(3). No third edge length.
  This reproduces the published "33 vertices and 113 edges" exactly.
- Exact arithmetic in Q(sqrt3, sqrt5, sqrt15) using 4-tuples of integers over the common
  denominator 8; independently cross-checked over all 528 pairs with sympy.
- Every pair at distance 1 or sqrt(3) is an edge; no pair at any other distance is an edge.
- Diagnostic: the 33-point SET is not a strict two-distance set: it realises 39 distinct
  pairwise distances, and 415 of the 528 pairs are at neither 1 nor sqrt(3). This is
  expected under the source's Definition 1 (edge iff d in D). A strict two-distance set
  in R^2 has at most 5 points (Einhorn-Schoenberg 1966, which is ref. [1] of
  Exoo-Ismailescu), so no 6-chromatic strict two-distance set exists in the plane.
  The invariant consumed by a virtual-edge substitution is the EDGE length set, which is
  exactly {1, sqrt(3)}.

=== Stage 4: SAT ===
- 5-colouring UNSAT: Cadical195 (0.36 s) and Glucose4 (0.64 s).
- 6-colouring SAT: Cadical195, colouring validated against all 113 edges.
- Independent repeat from runs/two_distance.json: 5-colouring UNSAT (Cadical195).
- chi = 6.

=== Stage 5: output ===
- runs/two_distance.json written: n=33, m=113, d2_values=["sqrt(3)"],
  non_unit_edges = the 32 length-sqrt(3) edges, chi=6, exact point strings,
  the raw Table 1 integer encoding, full edge list, unit edge list, 6-colouring.
- Independent audit (runs/two_distance_audit.py): all checks pass; max float
  edge-length error 9.7e-13; graph connected; degrees 5..11.
- Script runtime 6.9 s wall (first run 2.7 s), exit 0.

=== 2026-11-02 Result ===
OBTAINED: De Neve et al. 33-vertex 6-chromatic two-distance graph, distances 1 and sqrt(3),
81 unit edges + 32 non-unit edges. Coordinates: Appendix Table 1. chi=6 verified by SAT.

